{"content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT: `e1FixedOverheadTokens = 5500` is an unvalidated guess, and the static guard asserts the model — not the system — so it passed while reality was ~2k over the trigger**

This constant was never measured on the stack this test actually runs (the PTY leg — the author's own PR body discloses it never ran during this fix; the 5500 comes from a headless harness whose wiring the author admits differs: "carries a LARGER overhead than the test config"). Measured reality on the PTY stack (fold receipt captured on a linux runner, this head): E1 basis **12342 tokens** with tail reads 8 × 615 = 4920 → **fixed overhead ≈ 7422, not 5500**. The gap (~1.9k) is exactly the margin the guard demands (`trigger − 1500`), which is why the guard's `expect(e1Floor, lessThan(shouldCompactTrigger - 1500))` passes green while the nightly keeps going red.

The guard is the right idea (round 1 asked for it) but as written it validates arithmetic over invented constants — it can never catch a wrong constant. Make it honest:

```dart
// Measured on the PTY stack (linux hosted runner), <date/run id>:
// E1 request basis N tokens with tail = 8 × perRead; fixed overhead =
// N − 8 × perRead. Re-measure whenever the scenario shape changes.
const e1FixedOverheadTokens = <measured>;
```

and keep the symbolic derivation (`hideProtectTailEntries * perReadTokens + e1FixedOverheadTokens`) — that part is good. A guard over a measured constant turns the next constant drift or estimator change into a gate-time failure with the arithmetic spelled out; a guard over a guessed constant rubber-stamps the bug that put this file in quarantine in the first place.
